# Revision

**Revision** is the updating of a model in the light of evidence. It takes
what was believed and what has been found, and returns what is believed next.
It is the step that closes every loop described in this vault. The word also
names the thing the step leaves behind, a recorded version of a document, and
in a vault kept under version control the two senses are the two ends of one
act.

| | Revision as an operation | A revision as a record |
|---|---|---|
| What it is | changing a model, theory or specification | one stored state of a card |
| Input | model and evidence | the previous state and a patch |
| Decided by | whoever evaluates the evidence | whoever accepts the patch |
| In the discussion | row of the operations table; $T_n\to T_{n+1}$ | "new revision in Git"; pinned revision |

## Description

### Lane one: the operation

In the table of operations, revision takes a model and evidence and returns an
updated model. In the [[reasoning-cycle]] it follows [[evaluation]]: a
prediction fails, and the hypothesis that produced it is changed.

The discussion's most developed case is the revision of a formal theory. A
mathematician recognises that a theory leaves a statement undecided, weighs
reasons from outside the theory, and adopts a new axiom:

$$T_0\subseteq T_1\subseteq T_2\subseteq\cdots,\qquad T_{n+1}=T_n+\{G_n\}.$$

Two things are said about this.

- **It is not safe by construction.** The progression is an idealised
  example, not a procedure that preserves consistency or truth for arbitrary
  theories. A revision can make things worse.
- **It never finishes.** Every consistent, effectively axiomatized,
  sufficiently strong stage is incomplete again. Revision is permanent.

The thesis behind this part of the discussion was that a human adding axioms
might be what makes a strange loop essential. The reply accepted the
construction and declined the necessity: a program can carry out the same
progression, so the reviser need not be a person.

### Revising the rules of revision

The step the discussion calls more interesting is one level up. A human or
agent may change not the theory but the way new axioms are chosen:

$$(T_n,R_n)\longrightarrow(T_{n+1},R_{n+1}),$$

where $R_n$ encodes the current criteria of revision. This is evolving
[[reflection]], and it is the sixth of the seven candidate definitions of a
strange loop: a system uses a self-model to revise future models, rules, or
criteria for revision.

The difference between the two levels is the difference this vault keeps
returning to. Revising a model in response to evidence is a
[[feedback-loop]]. Revising the criteria by which one revises requires the
criteria to be represented inside the system, where they can be inspected and
changed, and that is a crossing of levels.

### Lane two: the record

In the proposed document system, changes by a person or an agent enter as
revisions or patches, never as silent edits, and Git records each accepted
one. The discussion's formal model makes the operation explicit:

$$H_{n+1}=\operatorname{Integrate}(H_n,\operatorname{Eval}(H_n)).$$

$H_n$ is a revision in the second sense. Passing from $H_n$ to $H_{n+1}$ is a
revision in the first. Three rules from the discussion govern the passage.

- An evaluation reads a *pinned* revision, so that a card is never judged
  while it is changing.
- An output records the revision it was computed from.
- Generated content becomes a revision only after approval; until then it is
  inert output.

The essay describes the same passage in programming: observations from
execution lead to a revised specification, and in a system where
specification, program and agent instructions are all inspectable artifacts,
the system can revise its own specifications and the programs that produced
its results. The essay adds the questions this raises, of validation,
permissions, provenance, versioning and the limits of self-modification.

A recorded history is what makes the higher level possible. Criteria of
revision can only be revised if earlier revisions and the reasons for them
can be read back.

## Relations

- It is one [[cognitive-operation]] {relation: instance-of}: model and evidence in, updated model out.
- It closes each turn of the [[reasoning-cycle]] {relation: part-of}.
- It acts on what [[evaluation]] {relation: uses} returns.
- Applied to its own criteria it is evolving [[reflection]] {relation: uses}.
- Its formal case is the [[reflection-progression]] {relation: example-of}, one new axiom per stage.
- It is made necessary without end by the [[incompleteness-theorems]] {relation: uses}.
- By itself it gives only a [[feedback-loop]] {relation: is-a}, not a [[strange-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from}.
- In documents it is the Integrate step of [[render-integrate-execute]] {relation: part-of} and the closing step of the [[hmd-feedback-loop]] {relation: part-of}.
- The party who accepts a revision is the [[human-in-the-loop]] {relation: uses}, though a program may take that place.
- Its record is kept as described in [[git-as-memory]] {relation: uses}, with [[provenance]] {relation: uses} for each output.
- It bears on the question [[building-a-loop-in-hmd]] {relation: answers}: the loop is built when a card's revisions revise the rules for revising it.
- It bears on the question [[learning-the-formalism]] {relation: answers}, as one of the operations mapped there.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[reflection-progression]].** Theories extended one undecided statement at
  a time. It is revision stripped down to its formal skeleton, and it shows
  that the process has no last step.
- **[[evaluation]].** Assessment of a claim against a criterion. A revision is
  what an unfavourable evaluation calls for.
- **[[reflection]].** A system analysing its own process. It supplies the
  self-model without which only the theory, never the criteria, can be
  revised.
- **[[render-integrate-execute]].** Three operations kept apart in an
  executable document. Integration is the one that creates a revision, and it
  is gated by approval.
- **[[human-in-the-loop]].** A person who inspects and approves. In the
  discussion that person selects axioms and accepts patches, and is replaceable
  in principle.

